---
title: SPA router
summary: Opt-in client navigation for islands apps — intercept links, swap the body, keep chrome persistent.
related:
  - app/ppr
  - data-state/remote-functions
---

Drop `<ogygia.Router />` into your root layout and same-origin link clicks become client-side navigations: ogygia fetches the target page, merges `<head>`, swaps `<body>`, and updates history — no full reload, no Kit client bootstrap.

> **When to use.** Add the router when you want **app-like navigation** across islands pages — no white flash between pages, persistent chrome, prefetch on hover. It is entirely opt-in: without it, links are ordinary full-page loads (an MPA), which is perfectly fine for many sites. Reach for it once the between-page reload starts to feel heavy.

```svelte
<!-- +layout.svelte -->
<script>
  import * as ogygia from 'ogygia';
</script>

<ogygia.Router />
{@render children()}
```

It is opt-in and gradual: on routes that are still `csr = true`, Kit owns navigation and ogygia stays out of the way.

## Link prefetch

The router honors Kit's full preload grammar, so existing `data-sveltekit-preload-*` attributes just work:

```svelte
<div data-sveltekit-preload-data="hover">
  <a href="/docs/start/install">Install</a>
</div>
```

Values `eager` / `viewport` / `hover` / `tap` warm the page-HTML cache at the matching moment, with Kit's nearest-ancestor inheritance.

## Weaving server islands {#weave}

When you navigate into a page with several [server islands](/docs/islands/server-islands), the router prescans the incoming page and pulls **all** its `render: 'deferred'` load holes down **one** batch request — not a fetch per hole, no waterfall. It is automatic; there is no attribute to set.

The flush is out of order: each hole's HTML comes back the moment that region settles, so a fast region lands before a slow one regardless of source order. Content appears as it is ready. A [command that returns a region](/docs/data-state/held-regions) rides the same one-response path — a single flight that both mutates and repaints.

## Speculative prerender {#speculate}

Prefetch warms the *HTML cache*. **Speculation goes further: the browser fully prerenders the next page** — runs its JS, fills its islands, resolves its server-island holes — in a hidden tab, before you click. Click, and it is swapped in instantly, already interactive. Combined with [PPR static shells](/docs/app/ppr) the prerender is nearly free, so navigation feels like it has no cost at all.

Turn it on in the plugin config:

```ts
// vite.config.ts
ogygia({
  continuity: {
    speculate: 'hover'    // prerender on ~200ms hover dwell (moderate eagerness)
    // speculate: 'viewport'  // prerender as soon as a link scrolls into view (eager)
  }
});
```

It uses the native [Speculation Rules API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Speculation_Rules_API) — no library, no client cost. ogygia injects one rules script that matches same-origin path links, and it is feature-detected: where the browser has no support it does nothing (the router's own prefetch still applies).

Two things are never speculated: the signed region endpoint, and `rel="nofollow"` links. Beyond that, `data-ogygia-speculate` gives you a cascade — set `off` on a subtree, and `on` back on a single link (or subtree) inside it. `on` wins over `off`.

```svelte
<!-- turn a whole section off, re-enable one link inside it -->
<nav data-ogygia-speculate="off">
  <a href="/logout">Log out</a>          <!-- not prerendered (has a side effect) -->
  <a href="/inbox" data-ogygia-speculate="on">Inbox</a>  <!-- prerendered anyway -->
</nav>
```

Reach for `off` on links with side effects you do not want fired early (a logout, a one-time action), or when you are debugging navigation and want a cold click; reach for `on` to re-enable the ones inside that are safe.

## Keep layout chrome {#keep}

Mark a subtree with `data-ogygia-keep` and it survives navigations untouched — its DOM (and any island state, scroll position, or playing media inside) is relocated across the body swap instead of remounted. This is how a sidebar keeps its scroll position as you move between pages.

```svelte
<div data-ogygia-keep="sidebar">
  <SideNav />
</div>
```

The sidebar on this very site uses it — notice it does not flash or reset as you navigate.

## What it is not

The router does no script processing: `<script>` tags injected by a body swap do not execute (standard browser behavior). If you need code to run per navigation, use an island. Shallow routing (`pushState` / `replaceState`) is not supported without Kit's client — use `goto()`.

> `invalidateAll` is a soft seed refresh, not a navigation: it re-fetches the current page and refreshes data seeds in place, with no view transition, body swap, or island remount. See [Data & remote functions](/docs/data-state/remote-functions).

## Do / don't

- **Do** wrap persistent chrome (sidebar, player, header) in `data-ogygia-keep` so it keeps state across navigations.
- **Do** use the `data-sveltekit-preload-*` attributes you already know — they work unchanged.
- **Do** turn on `continuity: { speculate: 'hover' }` for app-like instant navigation — with PPR shells it is nearly free. Opt risky links out with `data-ogygia-speculate="off"` (and `="on"` to re-enable one inside).
- **Don't** expect injected `<script>` tags to run after a body swap; put per-navigation code in an island.
- **Don't** add the router if a plain MPA is enough — it is opt-in for a reason.


